<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/expert/polymer-chemistry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Polymers are chemistry's lego: <strong>small molecules linked into chains of tens of
    thousands</strong>, whose properties come from chain length, tangling and packing rather than
    exotic chemistry. Polyethylene is just petrochemical ethene repeated — and it is made at
    ~100 million tonnes a year. Nature ran the same playbook first: DNA, proteins, cellulose and
    rubber are all polymers.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the polymer logic chain — from monomer to material" widget="equationStep" config={polyCfg} />

  <h2>The families that run daily life</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Addition plastics:</strong> polyethylene (bags), polypropylene (bottles), PVC (pipes), polystyrene (foam) — cheap, inert, and the source of the microplastic problem because those same inert bonds resist everything, including decay.</li>
    <li><strong>Condensation polymers:</strong> nylon (1935 — the first fully synthetic fibre), polyester, PET, and Kevlar (stronger than steel by weight) — built by stepwise linking with water kicked out.</li>
    <li><strong>Elastomers:</strong> rubber's chains are coiled and cross-linked lightly — stretch and they uncoil, release and they recoil. Vulcanisation (Goodyear, 1839) adds the cross-links that stop the melt.</li>
    <li><strong>Biopolymers:</strong> cellulose (trees), starch, proteins, DNA — the same chain logic with information or structure encoded. Same chemistry, different purpose.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The honest ledger</h2>
  <p>
    Plastics changed medicine, food safety and transport — and ~9% of all plastic ever made has
    been recycled. The frontier is chemistry's cleanup: enzymes that digest PET (discovered in
    compost heaps, engineered since 2016), and designing polymers that <em>want</em> to be
    recycled — depolymerising back to clean monomers on demand.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Carothers (1935); Staudinger's macromolecule hypothesis (Nobel 1953); OECD Global Plastics Outlook.</p>
</TopicPage>
